  "summary": "Mountain-village silver of Dagestan: deeply chased, nielloed and gilded vessels, chains and arms.",
  "description": "Kubachi, a clustered stone village in the Dagestan mountains, has produced smiths for centuries — first for Caucasian arms and harness, later for exhibition-grade silver: great water vessels (kumgans), trays, chains and dagger hilts covered in dense engraving, niello blackening, gilding and inset stones. Soviet power turned the workshops into an artel whose pieces toured exhibitions; hereditary families still pass designs from father to son. Museum collections from Moscow to the Met hold Kubachi masters' work.",
  "culturalContext": "The origins of Kubachi silver arrived in the region with Persian traders around 2,000 years ago. In Persian chronicles of the 4th century, the village is called Zerihgeran or Zirihgheran ('armory'). The present-day name, Kubachi ('makers of weapons'), is of Turkish origin.\n\nAccording to Fatima Gadzhalova and colleagues (2019), metalwork in Dagestan was 'largely promoted by the natural raw materials presence', more precisely wool, wood, stone, clay, and iron ore. Located along the trade routes between South Caucasus, the Near and Middle East, and Russia, Dagestan even had access to supplies of materials necessary for jewellery, copper minting, and so on.\n\nIn the 19th century, the Russian emperors and high nobility commissioned Kubachi silver, including decorated daggers. Emperor Alexander III gave his British counterpart Queen Victoria knives from Kubachi.",
